Our tree has the pre-HDFS-16563 message: `"token " + formatTokenId(identifier) + " is expired, current time: ..."`. The upstream HDFS-16563 changed the message to `"Token has" + identifier.getRealUser() + "expired, current time: ..."` (with a formatting bug producing "Token has…expired" and "Token  has expired" — double space when realUser empty), which broke the YARN test expecting "is expired". YARN-11172 then fixed the message to `"Token " + identifier.getRealUser() + " has expired, current time: ..."` and updated the test to use LambdaTestUtils.intercept with "Token  has expired".

Hmm wait: "Token  has expired" with two spaces — because `identifier.getRealUser()` returns "" (empty), giving "Token " + "" + " has expired" = "Token  has expired". Yes.

Now: our tree doesn't have HDFS-16563 applied. The requirement says implement YARN-11172: "Expired delegation token requests should continue to fail as InvalidToken, and the returned message should clearly state that the token has expired." Acceptance: message "explicitly identifies the token as expired".

Hmm — should I change the AbstractDelegationTokenSecretManager message in our tree from "token … is expired" to "Token <realUser> has expired, ..."? That's part of the upstream YARN-11172 diff. But it changes the error message format — the upstream commit did change it (fixing the mangled HDFS-16563 text). Since our tree never got HDFS-16563's mangled text, applying YARN-11172's message change would alter a correct message "token X is expired, current time..." to "Token realUser has expired, current time...". 

The acceptance signal: "rejected with an InvalidToken error whose message explicitly identifies the token as expired" and "the returned message should clearly state that the token has expired". The requirement's "Desired outcome" strongly suggests the "has expired" phrasing. I'll implement the full upstream YARN-11172 change faithfully: change the message in AbstractDelegationTokenSecretManager to the fixed "Token " + realUser + " has expired..." form, and update TestClientRMTokens to use LambdaTestUtils.intercept(InvalidToken.class, "Token  has expired", ...).

Wait, but the checkToken in the upstream patch context uses `err` variable and `LOG.info` + `formatTokenId`. Our version throws directly without logging. Upstream at YARN-11172 time:

```java
    long now = Time.now();
    if (info.getRenewDate() < now) {
      err =
          "Token has" + identifier.getRealUser() + "expired, current time: " + Time.formatTime(now)
              + " expected renewal time: " + Time.formatTime(info.getRenewDate());
      LOG.info("{}, Token={}", err, formatTokenId(identifier));
      throw new InvalidToken(err);
